      "side": 1,
      "message": "I may be missing a case, but I think the `vma_pte_add_slice()` call this replaces (aosp/4312507) already restores the slice that the swap PTE mapped.\n\nAs far as I can see, every path that installs an anonymous PTE in a 4KB VMA goes through `vma_pte_add_slice()`, so the slice that `try_to_unmap_one()` records is always `vma_slice_offset(vma, address)`. And nothing moves a PTE to another slice of its 16KB window: `mremap()` keeps the slice since aosp/4312522 (as on aosp/4367608), `move_pages()` rejects a UFFDIO_MOVE that starts at another slice (aosp/4312528), and `move_compat_swap_folio()` only moves whole windows (aosp/4367974). Fork copies swap PTEs to the same address, and swapoff restores them in place.\n\n`remove_migration_pte()`, `restore_exclusive_pte()` and `p3s_swapin_map_sibling_slices()` (aosp/4379310) also rebuild the slice from the address, so with this patch the faulting PTE and the sibling slices restored with it would get their slice from different places. The bits themselves look safe to me: bits 4-5 are free in every arm64 swap PTE format, and fork, `mremap()`, UFFDIO_MOVE and `mprotect()` keep them.\n\nWould it be OK to drop this patch? `p3s_uffd_swap_pte_movable()` in aosp/4368005, the only later user of `pte_swp_slice()`, could compare `vma_slice_offset(src_vma, src_addr)` with `vma_slice_offset(dst_vma, dst_addr)` instead.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "2d3747b0ae9aac21693a00fb3cdd0ebec8018f3a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
